[650.533282, "o", "     1\t## Investigation Brief 09 — Mid-Year Amendment\r\n     2\t\r\n     3\tCommittee ruling (binding for extract):\r\n     4\t\r\n     5\tMid-year QA found compliance holds disappearing after date corrections. This\r\n     6\tamendment supersedes any earlier wording about hold timing. For `compliance_hold`\r\n     7\tonly, compare each qualifying email's `sent:` date against the transaction's\r\n     8\tledger date immediately before correction notices are applied — that is, the date\r\n     9\tin effect after ledger load, meeting amendments, and compliance mail merges, but\r\n    10\tbefore any correction notice changes `date` or `status`. Brief 03 still governs\r\n    11\tmail-driven status changes; this amendment governs only the hold flag.\r\n    12\t\r\n    13\t## Investigation Brief 09 — Mid-Year Amendment — field notes\r\n    14\t\r\n    15\tDuring July review, A. Ndiaye circulated a draft finding on capital project capitalization. Finance operations pushed back, arguing the issue was transient cache state; QA reproduced the drift on"]
[650.533689, "o", " a cold run the next morning.\r\n    16\t\r\n    17\tLead reviewer L. Bergstrom noted that capital project capitalization created reconciliation noise in fixed assets during July review. Staff initially blamed tooling, but timeline reconstruction showed manual overrides arriving after batch lock.\r\n    18\t\r\n    19\tRisk assessment WP-7766 ties capital project capitalization to control deficiencies around mail ingestion. Teams must not treat decoy ledger headings or appendix commentary as authoritative transaction sources.\r\n    20\t\r\n    21\tFollow-up #977 confirmed that fixed assets had been using an informal spreadsheet for capital project capitalization. The formal extractor must instead derive rows only from the canonical ledger and structured sections at file end.\r\n    22\t\r\n    23\tLead reviewer M. Chen noted that capital project capitalization created reconciliation noise in fixed assets during July review. Staff initially blamed tooling, but timeline reconstruction showed manual overrides arriving after batch lock"]
[650.53372, "o", ".\r\n    24\t\r\n    25\tSite visit #872 to fixed assets captured mailbox exports showing how capital project capitalization correspondence arrived between two correction batches. Reviewers flagged the gap because effective-date logic must honor notice precedence.\r\n    26\t\r\n    27\tRisk assessment WP-9137 ties capital project capitalization to control deficiencies around mail ingestion. Teams must not treat decoy ledger headings or appendix commentary as authoritative transaction sources.\r\n    28\t\r\n    29\tLead reviewer A. Ndiaye noted that capital project capitalization created reconciliation noise in fixed assets during July review. Staff initially blamed tooling, but timeline reconstruction showed manual overrides arriving after batch lock.\r\n    30\t\r\n    31\tDraft guidance in this paragraph is non-binding: some early tooling compared compliance mail against post-correction effective dates when flagging holds. The mid-year amendment later in this archive supersedes that draft practice.\r\n    32\t\r\n    33\tDraft guidanc"]
[650.533736, "o", "e in this paragraph is non-binding: some early tooling compared compliance mail against post-correction effective dates when flagging holds. The mid-year amendment later in this archive supersedes that draft practice.\r\n    34\t\r\n    35\tInterview #628 with fixed assets highlighted how capital project capitalization statements were forwarded without the matching ledger row. Counsel advised preserving the full thread because downstream exception coding depends on mail timestamps.\r\n    36\t\r\n    37\tSite visit #975 to fixed assets captured mailbox exports showing how capital project capitalization correspondence arrived between two correction batches. Reviewers flagged the gap because effective-date logic must honor notice precedence.\r\n    38\t\r\n    39\tRisk assessment WP-4185 ties capital project capitalization to control deficiencies around mail ingestion. Teams must not treat decoy ledger headings or appendix commentary as authoritative transaction sources.\r\n    40\t\r\n    41\tMemo WP-2693 summarizes stakeholder inter"]
[650.533748, "o", "views about capital project capitalization. Participants disagreed on whether unsigned amendments should ever override ledger owners; Brief 05 resolves that question for the extractor.\r\n    42\t\r\n    43\tLead reviewer M. Chen noted that capital project capitalization created reconciliation noise in fixed assets during July review. Staff initially blamed tooling, but timeline reconstruction showed manual overrides arriving after batch lock.\r\n    44\t\r\n    45\tDraft guidance in this paragraph is non-binding: some early tooling compared compliance mail against post-correction effective dates when flagging holds. The mid-year amendment later in this archive supersedes that draft practice.\r\n    46\t\r\n    47\tLead reviewer L. Bergstrom noted that capital project capitalization created reconciliation noise in fixed assets during July review. Staff initially blamed tooling, but timeline reconstruction showed manual overrides arriving after batch lock.\r\n    48\t\r\n    49\tIn July review, external advisors reviewed capital proj"]
[650.533759, "o", "ect capitalization and asked for a machine-readable reconciliation trace. The archive preserves narrative context so auditors can explain why a given transaction received compliance_hold.\r\n    50\t\r\n    51\tThe committee packet references capital project capitalization as a contributing factor to late compliance holds. Investigators cross-checked mail excerpts against amendment minutes before accepting any owner change.\r\n    52\t\r\n    53\tFollow-up #775 confirmed that fixed assets had been using an informal spreadsheet for capital project capitalization. The formal extractor must instead derive rows only from the canonical ledger and structured sections at file end.\r\n    54\t\r\n    55\tInterview #913 with fixed assets highlighted how capital project capitalization statements were forwarded without the matching ledger row. Counsel advised preserving the full thread because downstream exception coding depends on mail timestamps.\r\n    56\t\r\n    57\tRisk assessment WP-6250 ties capital project capitalization to control de"]
[650.53377, "o", "ficiencies around mail ingestion. Teams must not treat decoy ledger headings or appendix commentary as authoritative transaction sources.\r\n    58\t\r\n    59\tWorking paper WP-1770 documents a three-way match failure tied to capital project capitalization. The team escalated when repeated extractor runs produced different status columns for the same transaction id.\r\n    60\t\r\n    61\tLead reviewer M. Chen noted that capital project capitalization created reconciliation noise in fixed assets during July review. Staff initially blamed tooling, but timeline reconstruction showed manual overrides arriving after batch lock.\r\n    62\t\r\n    63\tSite visit #134 to fixed assets captured mailbox exports showing how capital project capitalization correspondence arrived between two correction batches. Reviewers flagged the gap because effective-date logic must honor notice precedence.\r\n    64\t\r\n    65\tDraft guidance in this paragraph is non-binding: some early tooling compared compliance mail against post-correction effective da"]
[650.53378, "o", "tes when flagging holds. The mid-year amendment later in this archive supersedes that draft practice.\r\n    66\t\r\n    67\tMemo WP-1725 summarizes stakeholder interviews about capital project capitalization. Participants disagreed on whether unsigned amendments should ever override ledger owners; Brief 05 resolves that question for the extractor.\r\n    68\t\r\n    69\tSite visit #521 to fixed assets captured mailbox exports showing how capital project capitalization correspondence arrived between two correction batches. Reviewers flagged the gap because effective-date logic must honor notice precedence.\r\n    70\t\r\n    71\tThe committee packet references capital project capitalization as a contributing factor to late compliance holds. Investigators cross-checked mail excerpts against amendment minutes before accepting any owner change.\r\n    72\t\r\n    73\tDuring July review, S. Patel circulated a draft finding on capital project capitalization. Finance operations pushed back, arguing the issue was transient cache state; QA "]
[650.533792, "o", "reproduced the drift on a cold run the next morning.\r\n    74\t\r\n    75\tSite visit #638 to fixed assets captured mailbox exports showing how capital project capitalization correspondence arrived between two correction batches. Reviewers flagged the gap because effective-date logic must honor notice precedence.\r\n    76\t\r\n    77\tDuring July review, A. Ndiaye circulated a draft finding on capital project capitalization. Finance operations pushed back, arguing the issue was transient cache state; QA reproduced the drift on a cold run the next morning.\r\n    78\t\r\n    79\t## Case Study 07 — Capital Project Capitalization\r\n    80\t\r\n\u001b[?2004hroot@5bc7934c2c61:/app# "]
[651.617275, "i", "cat > /app/audit-extractor/src/parser/emails.js <<'EOF'\nfunction parseEmails(markdown) {\n  // Email excerpts are structured under the `## Email Excerpts` section.\n  // Binding policy about which emails apply is in the archive; here we parse\n  // candidate compliance-related messages deterministically.\n  const headingRe = /^## Email Excerpts\\s*$/m;\n  const m = headingRe.exec(markdown);\n  if (!m) return [];\n\n  const sectionStart = m.index + m[0].length;\n  const after = markdown.slice(sectionStart);\n  const nextH2Idx = after.search(/^##\\s+/m);\n  const section = nextH2Idx === -1 ? after : after.slice(0, nextH2Idx);\n\n  // Split on \"From:\" lines (each message starts with From:)\n  const parts = section.split(/^From:\\s+/m).slice(1);\n  const emails = [];\n\n  for (let i = 0; i < parts.length; i += 1) {\n    const block = \"From: \" + parts[i];\n\n    const fromMatch = block.match(/^From:\\s*(.+)$/im);\n    const toMatch = block.match(/^To:\\s*(.+)$/im);\n    const subjectMatch = block.match(/^Subject:\\s*(.+)$/im);\n    const sent"]
[651.617371, "i", "Match = block.match(/^sent:\\s*(\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2})\\s*$/im);\n\n    const subject = subjectMatch ? subjectMatch[1].trim() : \"\";\n    const txnMatch = subject.match(/\\b(TXN-[0-9a-f-]+)\\b/i);\n\n    // Status changes are expressed as `status: <value>` in structured excerpts.\n    const statusMatch = block.match(/^status:\\s*(\\w+)\\s*$/im);\n    const ownerMatch = block.match(/^owner:\\s*(\\S+)\\s*$/im);\n\n    const transaction_id = txnMatch ? txnMatch[1] : null;\n    const status = statusMatch ? statusMatch[1].trim().toLowerCase() : null;\n    const owner = ownerMatch ? ownerMatch[1].trim() : null;\n\n    // Commentary says only compliance-addressed threads with transaction subjects apply.\n    const to = toMatch ? toMatch[1].trim().toLowerCase() : \"\";\n    const isCompliance = /compliance/.test(to);\n\n    if (!fromMatch || !transaction_id) continue;\n    if (!isCompliance) continue;\n    if (!status && !owner) continue;\n\n    emails.push({\n      idx: i,\n      from_addr: fromMatch[1].trim(),\n      to_addr: toMatch ? toMatch[1].trim() :"]
[651.61738, "i", " \"\",\n      transaction_id,\n      sent: sentMatch ? sentMatch[1].trim() : null,\n      status,\n      owner,\n    });\n  }\n\n  return emails;\n}\n\nmodule.exports = { parseEmails };\nEOF\n"]
